  <p>
    γ-MSH — gamma-melanocyte-stimulating hormone — is the melanocortin that refuses to
    fit the pattern. Most peptides in this family, led by <a href="/alpha-msh">α-MSH</a>,
    are broad-spectrum signals that touch several receptors and headline in pigment and
    appetite. γ-MSH is comparatively picky about its receptor, and its main claim to
    fame is somewhere else entirely: the control of sodium and blood pressure.<Cite n={1} />
  </p>

  <Figure
    caption="The salt loop. A high-sodium diet raises pituitary γ-MSH; γ-MSH acts through MC3R to increase sodium excretion, and blood pressure holds. Remove MC3R, remove the enzyme that makes γ-MSH, or block the receptor, and the same diet produces salt-sensitive hypertension."
    source="Schematic, after Ni et al. 2003, Ni et al. 2006 (J Hypertens), Kathpalia et al. 2011 and Humphreys 2004."
  >
    <SaltLoop />
  </Figure>

  <h2>Three peptides from the quiet end of POMC</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/system">POMC</a> is cut into its hormones at pairs of basic amino acids,
    and the melanocortins come from three different stretches of it. α-MSH is carved
    from within ACTH in the middle; <a href="/beta-msh">β-MSH</a> from β-lipotropin at
    the C-terminal end; and γ-MSH from the N-terminal fragment, the part of the
    precursor that was for years regarded as biologically silent.<Cite n={3} /> Three
    versions exist, cut at different points of that fragment: γ1-MSH, an 11-residue
    amidated peptide; γ2-MSH, one residue longer; and γ3-MSH, a longer glycosylated
    form. All three carry the His-Phe-Arg-Trp core that defines the family, and the
    processing that releases them depends on prohormone convertase 2, the same enzyme
    that finishes α-MSH in the brain.<Cite n={3} /> The N-terminal fragment and its
    products are not quite silent at the adrenal, either: in 1982 Lowry's group showed
    that N-terminal POMC peptides stimulate adrenal cell division, and the longer
    γ3-MSH forms potentiate ACTH's steroidogenic effect — a supporting role to the
    receptor <a href="/receptors/mc2r">MC2R</a> that γ-MSH itself cannot
    activate.<Cite n={10} />
  </p>

  <h2>The receptor built for it</h2>
  <p>
    Where α-MSH activates four of the five receptors strongly, γ-MSH is relatively{' '}
    <a href="/receptors/mc3r">MC3R</a>-preferring. The pairing is written into the
    receptor's history: when MC3R was cloned in 1993 the paper described it as a
    receptor for γ-melanotropin, because it was the first melanocortin receptor that
    responded well to γ-MSH, and it was found in the hypothalamus and limbic
    system rather than in skin or adrenal.<Cite n={2} /> Thirty years later the cryo-EM
    structure of MC3R bound to γ-MSH showed why: beyond the shared core, the
    peptide's C-terminal tail reaches into a groove specific to MC3R that α-MSH
    cannot use, while at the neighbouring MC5R the same tail barely makes
    contact.<Cite n={11} /> γ-MSH has only weak activity at MC1R, MC4R and MC5R and
    none at MC2R, so it drives little pigment or satiety signalling on its own — the
    activity grid is laid out in the <a href="/binding-matrix">binding matrix</a>.
    That selectivity is exactly why MC3R and γ-MSH are discussed as a pair: the
    receptor's quieter, harder-to-pin-down physiology and its most selective ligand
    belong to the same story.
  </p>

  <h2>A job in salt</h2>
  <p>
    γ-MSH's distinctive role is in <strong>sodium balance</strong>. Infused into rats
    at doses that leave blood pressure and kidney blood flow unchanged, it increases
    sodium excretion — a natriuretic effect that does not run through the usual
    haemodynamic routes.<Cite n={4} />
    The physiology behind it is a feedback loop. Eating more salt raises the
    pituitary's processing of POMC to γ-MSH and the level of γ-MSH in the blood;
    γ-MSH, through MC3R, drives sodium out; blood pressure stays
    normal.<Cite n={1} /> The decisive test was to break the loop. Mice lacking MC3R,
    and mice lacking prohormone convertase 2 and therefore unable to make γ-MSH,
    have normal blood pressure on an ordinary diet but become markedly hypertensive
    on a high-salt one. In the PC2-deficient animals, infusing γ-MSH brought the
    pressure back down; in the MC3R-deficient animals it could not, because the
    receptor was gone.<Cite n={5} /> A high-salt diet also raises MC3R abundance in
    the rat kidney, as if the system turns up both the signal and the
    receiver.<Cite n={6} />
  </p>
  <p>
    Where the receptor actually sits is less settled than the loop itself. The
    natural assumption was MC3R on kidney tubules, inhibiting sodium reabsorption
    directly. But a careful look found neither MC3R protein nor its messenger RNA
    in mouse kidney, and γ-MSH had no effect on sodium transport in collecting-duct
    cells, which points instead to MC3R in the nervous system acting on the nerves
    that control the kidney.<Cite n={7} /> The loop is real; the wiring diagram is
    still being drawn.
  </p>

  <h2>The pressor paradox</h2>
  <p>
    At the same time, γ-MSH can <strong>raise</strong> blood pressure. Injected
    directly into the brain of mice it increases blood pressure and heart rate by
    stimulating sympathetic outflow — but this is not an MC3R effect. It happens
    just as strongly in mice lacking MC3R, and it is abolished by benzamil, an
    amiloride analogue, which points to γ-MSH acting on an amiloride-sensitive
    sodium channel in the brain rather than on a melanocortin receptor. Central
    α-MSH raises pressure too, but through MC4R, the route the obesity drugs have
    had to contend with.<Cite n={8} /> So the same peptide lowers pressure through
    its receptor in the periphery and raises it through a non-receptor route in
    the brain. The two findings come from two different experiments — peptide
    infused into the circulation versus peptide injected into the ventricles — and
    the reviews that weigh them treat the salt loop as the physiological role and the
    central pressor effect as a pharmacological one whose relevance at natural
    concentrations is unproven.<Cite n={12} />
  </p>

  <h2>Why there is no γ-MSH drug</h2>
  <p>
    The loop is a plausible drug target: salt-sensitive hypertension is common,
    and a stabilised γ-MSH analogue given to rats on a high-salt diet prevented the
    rise in blood pressure.<Cite n={9} /> Yet no γ-MSH-based medicine has entered
    the clinic. The reasons are the usual ones for a peptide hormone — short
    half-life, the difficulty of dosing a loop rather than a switch — plus the
    unresolved question of where MC3R acts and the knowledge that the peptide
    does the opposite thing centrally. MC3R has found its way into the{' '}
    <a href="/pipeline">pipeline</a> by another door: dual MC3R/MC4R agonists for
    obesity, which exploit the receptor's role in energy partitioning rather than
    salt. γ-MSH's own pharmacology, meanwhile, remains an unusually clean piece of
    physiology waiting for a use.
  </p>

  <h2>The honest bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    γ-MSH is the family's specialist in the way ACTH is: it took the melanocortin
    chassis and pointed it at a different problem. It is a useful correction to the
    idea that these peptides are interchangeable — the receptor a melanocortin
    prefers, and the tissue it acts in, can change its job entirely — and a reminder
    that "melanocortin" names a family of related signals with genuinely different
    physiological work. For the receptor at the centre of γ-MSH's biology, see{' '}
    <a href="/receptors/mc3r">MC3R — energy partitioning</a>.
  </p>

